  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The Global Horizon has shown clear scholarly uptake, with 15 citations in OpenAlex and a field-normalized percentile of 0.67458987, alongside 1,019 OAPEN downloads that indicate sustained reading interest. Its thematic alignment with Reduced inequalities also points to policy-relevant resonance, especially for work on migration, globalization, and unequal horizons of possibility.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2021, The Global Horizon has established a visible presence in scholarship on migration and globalization. The record shows 15 OpenAlex citations, which is a meaningful level of academic uptake for a recent scholarly book, and the citing literature is concentrated in migration studies, anthropology, sociology, and related social-science fields. This pattern suggests that the book is being used as a conceptual reference point for researchers examining migration aspirations, mobility, and the social production of opportunity across local and transnational settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage signals reinforce that this is not only a cited work but also a read work. OAPEN reports 1,019 downloads, indicating active engagement with the book in open-access form. The availability of the title through open repositories likely supports its circulation across institutional and geographical boundaries, which is consistent with the book\u2019s own focus on global horizons and unequal access to possibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is broad rather than confined to a single venue or national context. Citing works appear in journals across Europe and North America and include articles, book chapters, and a report, with especially strong links to migration and labor dynamics, refugees and integration, and diaspora and transnational identity. That spread suggests the book is being drawn into multiple scholarly conversations rather than remaining within a narrow disciplinary niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The subject profile also aligns with the Sustainable Development Goal Reduced inequalities, underscoring the book\u2019s relevance to questions of structural disparity, mobility, and social justice. Overall, the record portrays a book with solid scholarly traction, especially in migration-focused research, and with open-access distribution that has supported substantial reading beyond its immediate citation base.</p>",
